The Scaling Up Agricultural Technologies Program pursues a more intensive and grounded technology scaling and transfer initiatives wherein tailor-fitted interventions per selected area or community will be provided based on identified needs of the stakeholders. In this bottom-up process, the stakeholders are expected to proactively participate and contribute from the planning, packaging, implementation, monitoring, and evaluation of the program, projects, and activities that will be designed. The program employs the following components:
1) Sustainable Community-Based Action R4DE for Livelihood Enhancement, Upliftment, and Prosperity;
2) National Soil Health Program;
3) Community-based R4DE Program for Farm Productivity and Sustainability;
4) Agriculture Technology Business Incubation; and
5) Technology Commercialization.

The Institutional Development Program supports the acquisition of scientific and information communication technology equipment, as well as renovation and upgrading of R4D facilities (ie. food processing laboratory, and disease diagnostic laboratory), and basic R4D support facilities. This also includes the provision of scholarship support to DA RDE personnel to pursue master's and doctorate degrees in the field of agriculture and allied courses. The program employs the following components:
1) Human Resource Development;
2) Research Facilities Enhancement and Upgrading;
3) Strengthening the A/F Resources, Research and Extension for Development Network; and
4) Establishment of the A&F R&D Innovation Hub.

Specific Objective:
sustain operations of the IP-TBM of Guimaras State University;
craft and adopt spinoff policy; enhance Technology Transfer Protocol and IP policy of Guimaras State University;
integrate the enhanced IP policy and technology transfer protocol in the University's Research, Extension, and Thesis and Dissertation Manuals; intensify technology promotion and commercialization activities; and
increase and sustain linkages with various agencies on intellectual property protection management, and technology transfer and commercialization.

The two (2)-year PCAARRD funded project on Establishing the Intellectual Property and Technology Business Management (IPTBM) Operations at Biliran Province State University (BiPSU) under the Regional Agri aqua Innovation System Enhancement Program of
Eastern Visayas, was able to accomplish and establish the following:
1.) Capacitated three (3) BiPSU research and innovation personnel in four (4) major masterclasses namely; Intellectual Property Masterclass, Agribusiness Masterclass, Technology Commercialization and Mentorship Series and Technology Promotion and Mentorship Series.
2.) Capacitated at least 20 staff of the university in Intellectual Property Management through the conduct of re-echo seminars;
3.) one (1) inventory of matured technologies, twenty-two (22) prior art search reports, and the filing of over seventy (70) intellectual property applications and twelve (12) copyright submissions.
3.) Successfully established initial partnerships with business entities;
4.) one (1) pre commercialization report;
5.) two (2) technologies initially negotiated for commercialization; and,
6.) successfully pitched two (2) technologies during pitching events.

The Steel Building (formerly the “Foundry Building”) is a core R&D and innovation facility of the Metals Industry Research and Development Center (MIRDC), enabling industry productivity, technology development, and skills training in metalcasting. It houses essential metalcasting equipment and pilot-scale processing areas that support the development, testing, and prototyping of steel and metal-based products for local industries, SMEs, and government partners.

The project entails the retrofitting and modernization of the Steel Building to sustain its role as a functional R&D/innovation workshop and technology deployment hub. Key components include:
• Structural reinforcement to safeguard high-value R&D equipment and ensure uninterrupted laboratory and production operations;
• Rehabilitation of laboratories, pilot areas, training rooms, and technical workspaces required for product testing, casting, alloy formulation, and metal processing research;
• Upgrading of electrical systems, including installation of a new LVSG, generator set, and transformer to meet the power demands of advanced R&D processes and industrial-grade machinery.

This initiative supports the PDP 2023–2028 by strengthening science, technology, and innovation infrastructure, enhancing industry competitiveness, and improving resilience of critical public-service facilities. The modernized Steel Building will expand MIRDC’s capacity to deliver contract research, technology prototyping, casting product localization, and SME support through a time-sharing scheme—thereby generating broader socio-economic benefits and advancing national industrial development.

This project focuses on addressing the vital pressing issue that the tilapia industry is currently encountering which are, first, on the limited, unavailability and inaccessibility of tilapia grow-out operators to quality tilapia fingerlings, and second, outdated hatchery operations protocols and procedures not adjusted to cope up with the world’s changing climatic conditions. These gaps were raised during the Tilapia Industry Consultation Meeting spearheaded by the Inland Aquatic Resources Research Division of the Philippine Council for Agriculture, Aquatic and Natural Resources Research and Development last January 2024, and were also identified as weaknesses and threats of the Tilapia Industry in the Tilapia Industry Roadmap 2022 – 2025 by the Bureau of Fisheries and Aquatic Resources.

The use of quality tilapia breeds, and applications of optimized / refined hatchery protocols dictates and of important considerations on the sustainability and success of any tilapia grow-out operation. The project is composed of 4 major components which include the following: 
a) Component 1- Upscaling of parental broodstock production at CLSU Breeding Nucleus for distribution to accredited satellite breeding nucleus and FaST hatchery operators to augment the mass production and FaST fingerlings dissemination of the private sector with hatchery protocol refinements to update the current practices and cope up with the climate change; 
b) Component 2 - Crafting, establishing and implementing accreditation system for small to medium-scale FaST hatchery operators and FaST broodstock; 
c) Component 3 - Establishment of new and/or capacitating existing small to medium-scale FaST hatchery operators qualified for the accreditation system; 
d) Component 4 - FaST marker identification (SNP) for quality control of broodstock during the re-accreditation of tilapia hatcheries. 

At the end of the project, it is envisioned that the CLSU FaST Breeding nucleus will be having a major role in ensuring availability of quality tilapia breed to every tilapia grow-out operators.

General:	To develop a mapping system for visually representing the food system in the local setting, and utilize demand forecasting techniques based on production, food consumption data, and population growth projections.

Specific:
1. 	Enhance the competence and resources of researchers on food system analysis and GIS technology;
	2. 	Develop data collection system with georeferencing capabilities for conducting food environment survey;
	3. 	Develop a GIS-based platform to visually represent the local food system;
	4.	Map the density and characterize the type of producers within the local food environment context;
	5.	Develop a demand forecasting model focused on selected food items, utilizing data from production, intake, population growth projections to view present and predict future demand; and
	6.	Validate the effectiveness of system demand forecasting features through simulation with real-world data.
